# SET-2: Git Commands, Branching/PRs & Resume CI/CD

### 1. Importance of SCM & 15+ Essential Git Commands

**Importance:** Source Code Management (SCM) tracks every modification to code, enables parallel collaboration without overwriting teammates' work, maintains an audit history, and provides instant rollback capability if a bug is introduced.

| # | Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `git init` | Initializes a new local Git repository. |
| 2 | `git clone <url>` | Downloads a remote GitHub repository to local machine. |
| 3 | `git config --global user.name "Name"` | Sets author identity for commits. |
| 4 | `git status` | Displays modified, untracked, and staged files. |
| 5 | `git add .` | Stages all modified/new files for commit. |
| 6 | `git commit -m "Message"` | Saves a permanent snapshot of staged changes. |
| 7 | `git log --oneline` | Displays concise commit history and commit hashes. |
| 8 | `git diff` | Shows unstaged line-by-line code differences. |
| 9 | `git branch <branch-name>` | Creates a new isolated branch. |
| 10 | `git checkout -b <branch-name>` | Creates and switches to a new branch immediately. |
| 11 | `git merge <branch-name>` | Merges changes from specified branch into current branch. |
| 12 | `git remote add origin <url>` | Links local repository to a remote GitHub URL. |
| 13 | `git push -u origin main` | Uploads local commits to remote GitHub branch. |
| 14 | `git pull origin main` | Fetches and merges latest remote changes into local branch. |
| 15 | `git stash` | Temporarily shelves uncommitted changes for later use. |
| 16 | `git reset --hard <commit-id>` | Reverts working directory and history to a specific commit. |

